  "prediction": "**Portland Water Crisis: 38 Million Gallons of Drinking Water Flushed Due to Alleged Urination Incident**\n\nA controversy has erupted in Portland, Oregon, after city officials decided to drain 38 million gallons of drinking water from the Mt. Tabor Reservoir system due to an alleged incident of urination in the water supply. The decision has sparked debate and criticism from residents, experts, and national media.\n\nAccording to surveillance video, 18-year-old Dallas Swonger was seen urinating near Reservoir 5 early Wednesday morning. However, Swonger has denied the allegations, claiming that he actually urinated on the wall next to the reservoir, not in the water. In an exclusive interview with Vocativ, Swonger expressed his frustration and disappointment with the city's decision to flush the water, citing concerns about the environmental impact and the waste of a significant amount of water.\n\nCity officials, led by City Commissioner Nick Fish, have maintained that the decision to drain the water was made to ensure public health and safety, despite test results showing no signs of water contamination. Anna Harding, co-director of the School of Biological and Population Health Sciences at Oregon State University, has questioned the necessity of draining the water, stating that the risk of health risks is \"slight at best.\"\n\nThe incident has also sparked debate about the city's water management policies and the effectiveness of the Water Bureau. Kent Craford, a former lobbyist for large industrial water customers and co-petitioner of a ballot measure to switch control of the city's water away from the City Council, has criticized the decision as a \"public relations decision\" rather than a necessary public health measure.\n\nThe controversy has drawn national and international attention, with many questioning the city's decision to flush 38 million gallons of water. The incident has also raised concerns about the city's handling of the situation and the potential consequences for Swonger, who has been cited for trespassing and public urination.\n\nAs the situation continues to unfold, residents and experts are calling for a more nuanced approach to addressing the issue, one that balances public health and safety concerns with the need to conserve water and minimize waste.",
